+### JSON Patch — `applyPatch(document, patch)`
+
+Applies a [JSON Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902) — an array of the six operations of
+RFC 6902 § 4, each naming what it acts on with a JSON Pointer — and returns the patched document.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { items: ['disk'], count: 1 };
+
+applyPatch(document, [
+  { op: 'test', path: '/count', value: 1 },      // only go on if the count is still 1
+  { op: 'add', path: '/items/-', value: 'tape' },// "-" appends to an array
+  { op: 'replace', path: '/count', value: 2 },
+  { op: 'copy', from: '/items/0', path: '/main' },
+  { op: 'move', from: '/main', path: '/primary' },
+  { op: 'remove', path: '/items/0' },
+]); // { items: ['tape'], count: 2, primary: 'disk' }
+
+document; // unchanged: { items: ['disk'], count: 1 }
+```
+
+The result is a **new value**, built beside the document: neither `document` nor `patch` is changed,
+and nothing is shared between the three, so each can be changed afterwards without affecting the
+others. A patch is also all or nothing (RFC 6902 § 5) — if any operation fails, nothing is applied.
+
+The patch is validated at run time, so one parsed from JSON text can be passed in as it is. The
+`JsonPatch` and `JsonPatchOperation` types are exported for callers who want their own patches
+checked as they write them.
+
+It throws a `JsonPatchError`, whose `reason` tells the three failure modes apart:
+
+| `reason`           | when                                                                          |
+| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `invalid-patch`    | the patch is wrong whatever the document (unknown `op`, no `path`, bad pointer) |
+| `operation-failed` | the patch is valid, but does not fit this document (`remove` of a missing path) |
+| `test-failed`      | a `test` operation did not hold: the precondition the patch asked for is false  |
+
+```ts
+import { JsonPatchError, applyPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+try {
+  applyPatch(document, patch);
+} catch (error) {
+  if (error instanceof JsonPatchError && error.reason === 'test-failed') {
+    // ... the document changed under us: read it again and retry
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Points RFC 6902 leaves easy to get wrong, and which this implementation observes:
+
+- `add` inserts into an array, shifting the elements after it, and only replaces when the target is
+  an object member; its index may be one past the last element, which is what `-` means, while the
+  parent must already exist — `add` never creates the objects on the way;
+- `move` is a `remove` followed by an `add` (§ 4.4), so inside one array the destination index is
+  counted after the element has been taken out: moving `/0` to `/2` in `[1,2,3]` gives `[2,3,1]`;
+- `test` compares values as § 4.6 defines it: object members in any order, array elements in order,
+  and never across types, so the string `"10"` does not equal the number `10`;
+- a `test` on a location that holds nothing fails rather than erring: it is a question, and a
+  document holding nothing there is an answer of "no";
+- members an operation does not define are ignored (§ 4), so an `add` carrying a `from` is not an
+  error, and the `from` plays no part.
+
+Two operations are rejected as `invalid-patch` because no document could satisfy them: removing or
+moving the whole document (`path: ""`, which would leave no document at all), and moving a value
+into one of its own children (§ 4.4).
+
