+
+# Shape and semantics of `applyPatch`: errors, immutability and RFC 6902 strictness
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` is the second building block of the library and the first that
+*changes* a document. RFC 6902 specifies the six operations precisely, but leaves to the host
+language everything a caller will actually hold on to: how a failure is signalled and how finely,
+whether the patched document is the same value as the one handed in, and what to do with the handful
+of operations the RFC describes without saying which error they are. `dl-001` settled those
+questions for reading a document; this settles them for changing one, reusing its answers where they
+carry over.
+
+A patch is also the first input of the library that arrives from outside the process: documents are
+`JsonValue` by contract, while a patch is typically a payload parsed from a request body.
+
+## Decision
+
+**A second error class, `JsonPatchError`, with three reasons.** Failures throw
+`JsonPatchError extends Error` carrying `reason: 'invalid-patch' | 'operation-failed' |
+'test-failed'`. `dl-001` has the later features "reuse `JsonPointerError` and its `reason`"; what is
+reused is the *parsing and evaluation* of pointers, not the error a caller catches. `applyPatch`
+throws one class only, because a caller should not have to catch two to call one function, and a
+pointer failure inside an operation is a fault of the patch, which `invalid-patch` already names;
+the `JsonPointerError` that explains it is kept as the standard `cause`.
+
+The three reasons are the three answers a caller acts on differently — a service answering an HTTP
+`PATCH` maps them to 400, 409 and 412 — and each is a distinct question: is the patch wrong, does it
+not fit this document, or did the sender's own precondition turn out false?
+
+**`invalid-patch` is what can be decided from the patch alone; `operation-failed` needs the
+document.** That line, and not the RFC's list of `MUST`s, is what decides a reason. So an unknown
+`op`, a missing `path`, a `path` that is not a valid JSON Pointer, a missing `value`, moving a value
+into one of its own children (§ 4.4), and removing or moving the whole document are all
+`invalid-patch`: no document would make them succeed. A missing parent, an array index out of range,
+a token that is no array index of an array, a `remove` of what is not there: `operation-failed`. This
+follows `dl-001`, where a pointer that is both malformed and unresolvable reports `invalid-pointer`
+— what the input alone decides is decided first, and the whole patch is validated before the first
+operation runs.
+
+**A failing `test` is `test-failed`, including when its location holds nothing.** `test` is a
+question the sender chose to ask, not an accident, which is why it gets its own reason. A location
+that holds no value answers it with "no" rather than erring: a patch that tests `/a` is asking
+whether the document holds that value there, and one that holds nothing there does not.
+
+**`remove` and `move` with an empty path are rejected rather than given a meaning.** The RFC defines
+neither; a document cannot be absent, and `applyPatch` returns a `JsonValue`, so there is nothing to
+return. Inventing `null` would silently lose data.
+
+**The result is a deep copy, mutated as the operations are applied.** `applyPatch` copies the
+document once, applies the operations to the copy in place, and returns it only if all of them
+succeed. One mechanism answers three requirements at once: the result is a new value; the input is
+never touched, whatever happens halfway; and the all-or-nothing rule of § 5 needs no rollback, since
+a failed patch is simply a copy that is thrown away. Values taken from the patch are copied as they
+are inserted, so the result shares nothing with the patch either, and the same `value` used twice
+yields two independent values.
+
+The alternative, sharing the subtrees no operation touches, was rejected: it is faster on large
+documents, but then changing the result would change the document it came from, which is exactly the
+surprise the "new value" promise exists to prevent. The cost is a copy of the whole document per
+call, which is the right trade for configuration files and API payloads.
+
+**Object members are written with `Object.defineProperty`, never by assignment.** `dl-001` has
+pointers resolve own members only, so a document may hold a member named `__proto__` — `JSON.parse`
+creates one as an ordinary own property. Assigning that name would set the copy's prototype instead
+and lose the member; defining it keeps the document a plain record of its members, and a patch
+cannot reach any object's prototype.
+
+**The patch is validated at run time, and its type is `JsonPatch | JsonValue`.** A patch usually
+arrives as parsed JSON, and `JsonValue` is the library's currency, so it must be passable without a
+cast; a caller who writes a patch in TypeScript wants `JsonPatch` to check it instead. The union
+accepts both — a `readonly` array of operations is not a `JsonValue`, so one member does not subsume
+the other — and the run-time validation is what the implementation actually relies on.
+
+**Only the top level of a `value` is validated, not its inside.** A `value` member that is absent,
+or holds `undefined`, is an `invalid-patch`; a `JsonValue` that is not really one deeper down (an
+`undefined` nested in an object, a cycle) is not looked for. A patch that came from JSON text cannot
+hold either, and walking every value twice to protect against a caller who hand-built a bad one is
+not worth it. A cyclic `value`, like a cyclic document, overflows the stack.
+
+**Shared internals rather than parallel ones.** `json-pointer.ts` now exports `parsePointer`,
+`resolveTokens`, `arrayIndex`, `formatPointer` and `describeLocation` for the library's own use —
+`dl-001` foresaw the first of these — and a new `src/message.ts` holds the few helpers that render
+values in messages (`quote`, `describeType`, `count`, `preview`), so the two features name a type, a
+location and a value the same way. None of them is exported from `src/index.ts`: the public surface
+stays `applyPatch`, `JsonPatchError`, `JsonPatchErrorReason`, `JsonPatch`, `JsonPatchOperation` and
+what `dl-001` already published.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers catch two error classes if they both resolve pointers and apply patches, but only one per
+  function they call. A third reason added to `JsonPatchError` would break callers that switch on
+  `reason` exhaustively, as `dl-001` already noted for `JsonPointerError`.
+- Patching a large document costs a copy of all of it, every call. If a caller ever needs to apply
+  many patches to one big document, the place to look is a bulk entry point, not a change to this
+  one's promise.
+- Because the whole patch is validated first, a patch whose tenth operation is malformed does not
+  apply the first nine — which is also what § 5 requires of a patch that fails.
+- `invalid-patch` covers more than a reader of RFC 6902 § 5 might expect: moving a value into its own
+  child, and removing the whole document, are patch faults here, not failed operations. The README
+  says so, and the tests pin it.
+- The reasons are a published contract: `test-failed` in particular is what a caller matches on to
+  tell a lost race from a bad patch, so a failing `test` must keep reporting it even when the cause
+  is an absent location.
+- `src/message.ts` is where any later feature puts message rendering it shares, instead of copying
+  `quote` or `describeType` a third time.
