+
+# Implement JSON Patch `applyPatch` (RFC 6902)
+
+## Request
+
+Let the services change JSON documents with standard patches: implement JSON Patch as specified in
+RFC 6902, with its six operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`), exporting
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a patch to a document and returns the
+result as a new value, without mutating its input, and throws when the patch is invalid or one of its
+operations fails.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test first: `test/json-patch.test.ts` was written and run against a missing module (red), then the
+module was added until the suite passed (green), then the error messages and the internal seams were
+refactored with the suite green throughout.
+
+- `src/json-patch.ts` — `applyPatch`, `JsonPatchError`, `JsonPatchErrorReason`, and the
+  `JsonPatchOperation` / `JsonPatch` types. Validation (`preparePatch`) is separate from evaluation
+  (`applyOperation` and the four writers `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `test`), with `placeOf` locating
+  the container an operation writes in, `cloneJson` copying JSON values and `jsonEquals` comparing
+  them as § 4.6 defines.
+- `src/message.ts` — new: `quote`, `describeType`, `count` and `preview`, the helpers that render
+  values in error messages, shared by both features instead of copied.
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — unchanged in behaviour; `parsePointer`, `resolveTokens`, `arrayIndex`,
+  `formatPointer` and `describeLocation` are now exported for the library's own use (not from
+  `src/index.ts`), and `quote` / `describeType` moved to `src/message.ts`. `resolvePointer` is now
+  `resolveTokens(document, parsePointer(pointer))`.
+- `src/index.ts` — adds `applyPatch`, `JsonPatchError`, `JsonPatchErrorReason`, `JsonPatch` and
+  `JsonPatchOperation` to the public surface.
+- `test/json-patch.test.ts` — 73 tests: the examples of RFC 6902 appendix A, then each operation,
+  the patch as a whole, immutability, and the invalid patches.
+- `README.md` — a feature section with usage, the three failure modes, and the points of the RFC that
+  are easy to get wrong.
+
+Behaviour, following RFC 6902 § 4 (operations), § 4.6 (equality) and § 5 (error handling):
+
+- the patch is an array of operations, applied in order, each seeing the result of the ones before;
+- `add` replaces an object member, inserts into an array (index at most one past the last element,
+  `-` meaning the end), or replaces the whole document for `path: ""`; the parent must exist;
+- `remove` deletes the target, which must exist, closing the gap in an array;
+- `replace` overwrites a target that must exist; `path: ""` replaces the whole document;
+- `move` removes at `from` then adds at `path`, so indexes shift before the add; `copy` adds a deep
+  copy and leaves `from` alone;
+- `test` compares as § 4.6 does — object members in any order, array elements in order, nothing
+  across types — and fails rather than errs when its location holds nothing;
+- members an operation does not define are ignored (§ 4): an `add` carrying a `from` is not an error;
+- the document is deep-copied once, the operations change the copy, and it is returned only if all of
+  them succeed: the input is never touched and a failing patch changes nothing (§ 5).
+
+Failures throw `JsonPatchError` with `reason: 'invalid-patch' | 'operation-failed' | 'test-failed'`.
+The message names the operation by its position and its pointers, e.g.
+`JSON Patch operation 0 ("test" at "/count") failed: the document holds 1 there, not 9.`
+
+Appendix A.13 of the RFC (an operation object with two `op` members) is not reachable through this
+API: it is a duplicate key in JSON text, which `JSON.parse` resolves before `applyPatch` sees it.
+Everything it leaves — an operation whose `op` is unknown or missing — is tested.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 93 tests, 93 pass, 0 fail (20 for JSON Pointer, unchanged, 73 for JSON Patch).
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, sources and tests, `strict` on.
+- `npm run build` — clean; `dist/index.js` was imported and exercised with a six-operation patch,
+  confirming the `.ts` → `.js` specifier rewriting still holds with the new modules.
+- The README example was run as written, and prints what it claims.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- `dl-002-shape-and-semantics-of-applypatch-errors-immutability-and-rfc-6902-strictness` — the second
+  error class and its three reasons, the line between `invalid-patch` and `operation-failed`, the
+  copy-then-mutate approach to immutability and atomicity, writing members with
+  `Object.defineProperty`, the run-time validation of the patch, and the shared internals.
+
+## Follow-up for the next features
+
+- `src/message.ts` is the place for message rendering a third feature would otherwise copy.
+- A diff feature producing a patch can reuse `JsonPatchOperation` as its output type, and
+  `jsonEquals` (module-private today) as its comparison; export it from `src/json-patch.ts` when it
+  is needed outside.
+- If applying many patches to one large document ever matters, that calls for a bulk entry point,
+  not for weakening the promise that the result is a new value.
